refentry.refsect1:
&reftitle.description;
public arrayYac::dump intlimit100 intoffset0
Dumps metadata of the entries currently stored in the cache. The values themselves are not returned.

&reftitle.examples;
Yac::dump example
<?php $yac = new Yac(); $yac->set("foo", "bar"); $yac->set("baz", "qux");
print_r($yac->dump()); ?>
&example.outputs.similar;
Array ( [0] => Array ( [index] => 12345 [hash] => 14463105906481965911 [crc] => 0 [ttl] => 0 [k_len] => 3 [v_len] => 3 [size] => 0 [atime] => 1725955200 [hits] => 0 [kind] => 5 [loc] => 1 [enc] => 0 [key] => foo )
[1] => Array ( [index] => 12987 [hash] => 15132029420525657053 [crc] => 0 [ttl] => 0 [k_len] => 3 [v_len] => 3 [size] => 0 [atime] => 1725955200 [hits] => 0 [embedded] => 1 [key] => baz )
)
Entries are listed in slot order, not in the order they were stored. Values stored in the slot have crc and size set to zero. The kind, loc, and enc fields are available as of Yac 3.0.0.
Paging through a large cache
limit bounds how many entries a single call returns, and offset skips that many entries before collecting, so the two can be combined to page through a cache that holds more entries than one call may return.
<?php $yac = new Yac();
// fill the cache with 250 entries for ($i = 0; $i < 250; $i++) { $yac->set("key$i", $i); }
$page_size = 100; $page_num = 2;
// skip the first 100 entries and return the next 100 (page 2) $page = $yac->dump($page_size, $page_size * ($page_num - 1));
var_dump(count($page)); ?>
&example.outputs.similar;
int(100)
Fewer entries than requested are returned when the cache holds fewer entries than the requested page covers, and an empty array is returned once the offset points past the last occupied slot.
